{
  "case": "17-box-splice",
  "description": "A mantle appearing inside another mantle as one rune (README §7). The `player` mantle is a shirt worn on skin plus a voice; a rune of glyph `player` in the world IS that mantle, spliced in at its ports. What this case pins is the splice itself: the sub-net's agents enter the composed net namespaced by the RUNE (`p1/skin`, not `player/skin`, so two players in one world are two independent copies), the sub-net's own wiring survives, and an edge from the parent addressing box port k lands on the k-th port of the declared interface. Here the world wires a door to box port 0, which is `skin:0` — so after composition the door is wired to `p1/skin`, an agent the world never named. Nothing reduces: there are no rules, so the expectation is purely the composed shape. `pin_ids` is on so the NAMESPACING is pinned too, not just the shape: the canonical form is id-blind by design, so without it an implementation could splice correctly and name the agents anything. The ids are normative here — `<rune>/<agent>`, composing left to right when boxes nest.",
